arxism never died rather it  lived on as a specter continuing to  haunt western society for some that  specter is frightened a looming threat  with the potential to destroy western  capitalism for others that spectre is  mournful because they view marxism as a  ghost of lost potential a specter of a  world that could have been and the  future that could have been remains a  ghostly presence gone but never  forgotten aspects of the marxist dream  are seen in fits and starts but never  with the same momentum or clarity as in  the past like when bernie sanders ran a  presidential campaign that while  definitely not marxist was based on  dismantling certain structures of  neo-liberal capitalism in order to  facilitate greater equality we see  ontology alive and well in america in  the lingering nostalgia for the 1950s a  time supposedly defined by happy  housewives a booming economy affordable  housing and college education for all  white picket fences and leave it to  beaver seem infinitely more attractive  than what the present has to offer the  specter of the 50s thus leaves us  longing for america's glory days we see  the same mythologizing throughout elden  ring it's a world defined by its  destruction but even the most tragic  ruins you encounter seem to whisper  faint memories of past glory indeed this  was even embedded in the development  process george r.r martin was recruited  to build the world of elden ring's  glorious past then miyazaki and ko  brought that world to present day and  turned it nasty a castle with collapsed  towers evokes halcyon days of flying  banners and pageantry there's even a  secret boss fight where time rewinds and  a dragon returns to life to reassert its  power and glory remnants of an ancient  glorious past are everywhere in this  unglorious unwelcoming world and also  literal ghosts lots of ghosts haunting  us in the present so telling stories  about how great the past was is all well  and good but as with most storytelling  narratives about the past are typically  riddled with nostalgic half-truths  outright lies and everything in between  so when society creates a fabricated  version of the past what happens to the  real past that actually existed  according to darida the real less savory  version of the past sort of peeks  through society's curtains influencing  our world in subtle and not subtle ways  this is where we get the haunting part  of ontology as their real lived past  becomes the greatest ghost of all a  looming spectre haunting the present we  see this in the real version of the  1950s that continues to haunt america  racism was rampant and the redlining  that occurred continues to be realized  in unequal housing opportunities to this  day the decade also saw a major step  backwards for women in the workplace  which continues to be seen in a wage gap  that is alive and well everything from  the red scare to the genuine fear of  nuclear war to american foreign policy  decisions that wreaked havoc on south  america among other places it all gets  erased when we reminisce about america's  glory days still many of us living in an  objectively unappealing present look  back on this imagined past with  nostalgia the world of elden ring like  america has a narrativized past defined  by greatness but similarly the real past  haunts the present and suggests  something more nefarious and perhaps the  scariest ghosts in elden ring aren't the  ones lurking in creepy manners but the  world's real yet unspoken past there are  plenty of signs indicating that the  olden days were not as glorious as  everyone tells you hell if it had been  so awesome there would be no game what's  more there doesn't seem to be a  consistent vision of what the glorious  past actually was the enemies you fight  are people and creatures with opposing  motivations suggesting that the old  world wasn't so peaceful in fact it  seems to have been rife with factions  and organizations competing for power  prytile reichardt  [Music]  luna princess ronnie  willful traitors  all  it's also hinted that the legend of the  golden order supposedly supported by an  ineffable god has been given a glossy  varnish the rhythms and calculus of the  master's finger  betray a suspicion of the holism of the  golden order after all if everybody got  along so well why did things descend  into war and if the world was so perfect  why did marika decide to break it all  signs indicate that this perfect world  may have been more illusory than we're  initially led to believe how long will  it be i wonder  before the tarnished tire of a basins to  the two fingers  after all not everyone seems so big on  the whole golden order thing plenty of  characters are determined to destroy it  by burning the herd tree sure some of  those characters are horrifying like the  snake man  but some seem genuinely good like the  maiden who guides you on your quest she  even entreats you to burn down the tree  so i'd like you to undertake  a new journey  with me  to the flame of ruin  far above the clouds  upon the snowy mountaintops of the  giants  then  i can set the earth tree aflame an act  deemed the cardinal sin by others the  burning of the earth tree is the first  cardinal sin all of which suggests that  good and evil might not be so simple  here the idea of being haunted by the  past or some imagined version of it  might not be too surprising but when  writer mark fisher expanded on the idea  he identified something arguably more  disturbing when you're haunted by an  imagined past you're also haunted by  what fisher calls lost futures i.e  potential futures that could have  materialized if not for the events in  the past that closed those paths off he  cites philosopher franco berardi who  coined the phrase the slow cancellation  of the future this describes his view of  the way any hope for a happy future in  the west was eroded from the late 70s  into the 21st century as the rise of  neoliberalism attacked social welfare  lionized consumerism wreaked havoc on  the environment and so on as fisher puts  it the slow cancellation of the future  has been accompanied by a deflation of  expectations the feeling of belatedness  of living after the gold rush is as  omnipresent as it is disavowed the sense  of futurelessness in our own society can  best be realized by looking to the  comparative optimism of the 1950s back  then visions of the future were  starry-eyed just think of the jetsons  yet in our contemporary world this  vision no longer seems attainable the  vision of the future as imagined in the  50s can be considered lost yet it  continues to pervade our culture in the  form of retro-futurism i.e images and  culture technology and so on that evoke  what people in the 50s thought the  future would look like retro futurism  and culture continues to perpetuate the  jetson's vision of the future even as  that path now seems impossible such is  the power of ghost we see this too  throughout elden ring where characters  wax poetic about what could have been  had the world not been destroyed you  have nobles asking you to recapture  their lost castles daughters asking for  help finding their father even a weird  little man who just wants to live in his  cave again take the demigod radon who  was before the shattering a powerful  warrior and servant of the golden order  during the war he was infected with a  disease and driven mad in the midst of  the broken world of the lands between he  holds tournaments at a castle where  warriors can fight him for the ultimate  prize his great rune of the elden  [Music]  the  rings  is nigh  general radon  mightiest demigod of the shattering  awaits you clearly he can't let go of  his time as a warrior and ruler instead  continuing to fight trying to relive his  glory days in fact his fellow soldiers  from the war are continuing to  haphazardly fight though there's no  longer any real battlefield to fight on  clearly radon is in mourning for the  lost future where he would have  maintained his greatness so much as his  grief that he even uses his powerful  gravity magic to hold the stars in place  this keeps the world in stasis hiding  from a future where he actually has to  confront the loss and destruction of the  broken elden ring and only once you  defeat him can the stars move again so  clearly your quest within elden ring  isn't just to resurrect a lost past by  restoring the golden order it's much  bigger than that by putting the pieces  of the ring together and restoring that  past you're also reclaiming a lost  future as an extension of that glorious  pest but who benefits from this well  most of the characters urging you on are  servants or beneficiaries of the golden  order that includes both heroes and  villains who want to return to a past  they believe was taken from them but the  various endings which we'll detail more  later call the nobleness of this act  into question like many from software  games the majority of endings have you  reconstructing a version of the status  quo and instantiating a hollow version  of this lost future now underpinning the  idea of a world of lost futures is a  sense that we can no longer imagine any  kind of actually attainable future the  lost futures that haunt us simply loom  too large fisher identifies this lack of  imagination in his book capitalist  realism a term he defines as the  widespread sense that not only is  capitalism the only viable political and  economic system but also that it is now  impossible even to imagine a coherent  alternative to it though the world of  elden ring has nothing to do with  capitalism we do see a similar vibe one  of not being able to imagine an  alternative to the world in which you're  living take the demigod ranala she's  trapped in a library surrounded by her  creepy children and holding what appears  to be a squishy amber egg the game hints  that after ronald's daughter ronnie  killed herself ronald became obsessed  with this egg of rebirth  [Music]  rather than move beyond her loss into a  new future she is consumed by a desire  to recreate the daughter that she lost  she literally can't imagine an  alternative to her current situation did  not  come out say i  or will he be gravestones  to be better born a new fisher believes  that this stasis makes sense under  neoliberalism a system that is  unresponsive impersonal centerless  abstract and fragmentary in a world so  subsumed by capitalism a world that  imagines every person as primarily a  consumer everything is primarily a  product to be consumed  the prospect of change feels kind of  hollow and hopeless as he puts it  capitalism seamlessly occupies the  horizons of the thinkable fischer often  identifies this capitalist realest sense  that there is no future in our art and  culture he sees the endless postmodern  culture of remixing and remaking of  pastiche and nostalgia as evidence that  we have lost the capacity to imagine  anything that has not already been  created this dooms us to repeat the  structures of the past both in art and  in life  even the counterculture today he argues  endlessly repeat older gestures of  rebellion and contestation as if for the  first time and the concept of a society  stuck on a loop pervades elden ring far  more than you'd see in an average video  game the gameplay itself is remarkably  repetitive which is probably a good  thing given how hard it is in fact it's  basically defined by repetition  particularly with regards to death first  off you die like  a lot  no  me no but what's more when you die it's  not like in say mario where you just  reset and try again rather when you die  the runes you are carrying are lost  unless you go back and retrieve them  this forces you to return to sites of  defeat over and over again with  different approaches or better plans in  order to preserve your progress and grow  as both a player and a character each  time you know a little better what to  expect and possibly how to avoid yet  another death and that brings us to the  first not depressing part of this video  the possible emancipatory potential of  ontology and specifically of repetition  see being haunted by lost futures  doesn't doom us to a life of what ifs at  least not necessarily fisher also  believe that these ghosts can also  inspire us to see different potentials  for the world outside of our present  maybe that means imagining a world where  we don't have to rely on fossil fuels  that are slowly suffocating the planet  where you don't have to view yourself as  a sentient personal brand back in the  1960s philosopher gil de luz recognized  the potential of repetition as an active  force producing difference he saw  repetition as the power of beginning and  beginning again he was responding in  part to sigmund freud who saw repetition  as a psychological compulsion a need to  recreate a past that feels safe in its  familiarity conversely the luz inspired  by michael and kierkegaard understood  repetition as a springboard for  reinvention for conceiving of new  choices for creating spaces of  difference we see this idea of  repetition as reinvention at the core of  the game in its conclusion now most soul  games are  pretty depressing they often underscore  the futility of trying the false  illusion of choice in a universe where  most things are predetermined but not  elden ring at least not every time  notably the game offers several endings  and one everybody dies  bummer in most you restore the world  pretty close to how it used to be but  always with a wrinkle there's also one  where you burn literally everything down  in a frenzy of destruction but there's  one ending that we find really  interesting in it you restore the elden  ring but rather than handing it back to  marika and returning to the golden order  you give that power to ronnie remember  the the daughter who died by suicide but  is now back as a ghost who lives in a  doll like we said this game really is  all about haunting oh  a dogged fellow aren't we  or is it merely thy habit to talk to  dolls with this newfound power ronnie  changes the nature of the golden order  which was once a godlike force that  exerted power on and intervened in the  world under ronnie's rules the golden  order is still a system that like  gravity sets certain rules but now the  people within it have free will and can  abide by or violate its norms as they  see fit in this way a world once ruled  by predestination is now ruled by the  uncertainty that is individual choice  and choice as basically every  existentialist philosopher would tell  you is a necessary prerequisite for  freedom in this way ronnie has imagined  a different world one where visions of  the future are not dictated by myths of  the past and according to folks like  mark fisher that's what our real world  needs most people willing to conceive of  new possibilities rather than passively  accepting all the logical  inevitabilities of our present of course  if you want to reinvent the future you  have to let go of the mythologized  glorious past you were trying to restore  and accept the past as it actually was  that's the only way to stop being  haunted by it and to realize a different  future whether it's in a video game or  in the real world but what do you guys  think is elden ring a game about letting  go of the past or  are our brains just broken from trying  to understand derrida let us know what  you think in the comments a huge thanks  to all our patrons and if you haven't  checked out our patreon recently we have  a lot of very cool new stuff coming and  not in the imagined future sort of way  it's all really happening so please  check out our patreon page i think  you're gonna like what's going on set  fire to that subscribe button like  you've got a dragon head for a hand and  don't forget to ring that bell and as  always thanks for watching later  [Music]  [Applause]  [Musi